    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay that's a bit more helpful than the other stuff i've read. so if these \"trucks\" get stuck and start forming plaque, is it just a passive buildup? like, does the plaque just sit there, or is the process of it getting stuck and hardening actually an active, aggressive thing happening to the artery?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It's not just passive; it's a very active, ongoing process, which is why it's more complex than just a slow pile-up. When LDL particles get stuck, they don't just sit there like a pile of trash; they trigger an inflammatory response in the artery wall. This inflammation is where the real trouble starts, as it encourages immune cells to come and try and \"clean up\" the stuck LDL, but instead, they contribute to the plaque becoming unstable and growing. This process of inflammation and cellular activity is what makes it aggressive.",
      "source": "target",
